                <h1>SOP for Reconciling Overseas Ad Payments, Spend, and Invoices</h1>
<p>Reconciliation should begin when funds are paid, not only at the end of the month. Operations should own the account and spend records, while Finance should verify payments and invoices. Both teams should work from the same account list, reporting calendar, and discrepancy log.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Create a Unified Reconciliation Ledger</h2>
<p>Build the ledger by ad platform, account, currency, campaign owner, and legal entity. For every account, record the platform name, account ID, payer, currency, payment date, credit date, amount, and transaction reference.</p>
<p>Agree on three rules before reviewing data:</p>
<ul>
<li>Which time zone defines daily spend; normally, the platform's billing time zone;</li>
<li>Which exchange rate converts foreign currency into the reporting currency;</li>
<li>What rounding threshold the company accepts.</li>
</ul>
<p>The threshold should be approved in advance rather than changed during month-end close. Update the ledger weekly to avoid reconstructing history under deadline pressure.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Verify That Payments Have Credited the Correct Accounts</h2>
<p>Match the payment receipt, payment-channel statement, and platform credit record. Check not only the total amount, but also the payer, currency, partial credit, delayed credit, and rejected transactions.</p>
<p>Use this control formula:</p>
<p><strong>Closing available balance = Opening balance + Credited payments + Refunds restored - Confirmed spend - Platform adjustments</strong></p>
<p>If the result differs from the platform balance, flag the discrepancy rather than overwriting the source data. Common causes include automatic renewals, expired credits, coupons or rewards included in the displayed balance, and delayed crediting.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Reconcile Spend Using a Consistent Reporting Calendar</h2>
<p>Export spend details from each platform and compare them with the payment ledger and internal campaign reports. Pay particular attention to calendar-day versus billing-day differences, cross-time-zone attribution, refunds, and platform fees.</p>
<p>For example, a cross-border e-commerce team may operate accounts in USD and EUR. Its internal dashboard may use Beijing time, while the platform invoice uses another billing time zone. The correct approach is to restate the data by the platform's billing period before investigating duplicate charges or missing spend.</p>
<p>Link recognized spend to paid invoices. Charges incurred but not invoiced, and invoices issued but not yet charged, should remain in separate accrual or pending-review categories.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Match Platform Statements to Invoices</h2>
<p>Create an invoice register containing the invoice number, legal entity, tax ID, amount, tax, currency, issue date, payment status, and linked ad accounts. Match each invoice with the relevant platform statement by account and billing period.</p>
<p>Check whether:</p>
<ul>
<li>The invoiced entity matches the payer and the party operating the accounts;</li>
<li>All relevant accounts and billing periods are covered;</li>
<li>The gross amount, tax, and currency agree with the statement;</li>
<li>There are paid-but-uninvoiced, unpaid, or duplicated invoices.</li>
</ul>
<p>Because overseas platforms may use prepaid, postpaid, or monthly settlement models, the documentation requirements may differ. Keep the payment transaction, platform statement, and invoice linked through consistent reference numbers.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Complete the Monthly Close</h2>
<p>Set a fixed close date. Operations submits spend details, and Finance submits payment and invoice records. The review should:</p>
<ol>
<li>Lock the reporting period and exchange-rate method;</li>
<li>Reconcile opening balance, payments, spend, and closing balance;</li>
<li>Match statement amounts, invoice amounts, and settled payments;</li>
<li>Classify each discrepancy as pending credit, timing difference, exchange-rate difference, rounding difference, duplicate entry, or missing documentation;</li>
<li>Confirm available balances, unspent campaign budgets, and the treatment of refunds;</li>
<li>Archive supporting documents and obtain review sign-off from both owners.</li>
</ol>
<p>The final report should reflect both the financial expense and the remaining advertising balance. A ledger can be financially balanced while still leaving an ad account underfunded for upcoming campaigns.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Resolve and Track Discrepancies</h2>
<p>The discrepancy log should include a reference number, account, amount, reason, owner, and deadline. Before month-end close, prioritize balance issues that may interrupt campaigns, followed by duplicate charges, incorrect invoices, and entity mismatches.</p>
<p>Small rounding differences may be written off only after the reason is documented and the company threshold is met. Retain the exchange-rate source for currency differences. Delayed credits should be followed up with the platform and payment provider rather than recognized as losses. Escalate unresolved items that exceed the agreed service window.</p>
<p>After two or three consistent reporting cycles, the team can use actual exception patterns to adjust review frequency and reconciliation thresholds.</p>
